#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133646 :question/id #uuid "e121d861-51e8-578a-9bbe-a3f11f9d9452" :question/math-academy-id "e-6316" :question/problem "Find a definite integral that's equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$ given that $\\mathbf F(x, y) = xy \\,\\mathbf i - x^2\\,\\mathbf j$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf r(t) = t^3\\,\\mathbf{i} + t^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0, 1].$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int_a^b\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r (t))\\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = t^3, \\qquad y = t^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =({t}^{3})({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-({t}^{3})^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={t}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{t}^{6}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =({t}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{t}^{6}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3{t}^{7}-2{t}^{7} \\\\  & ={t}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{7}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e34a2cbf-0bed-50eb-9e62-7c6e55560cb9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954 :topic/id #uuid "cee13705-0b60-5ea0-b11e-46fd35bede4e" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059731}] :topic/math-academy-id 177 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :topic/title "Calculating the Dot Product Using Components"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999 :topic/difficulty 1.171346431943319 :topic/id #uuid "d21fb3a0-a806-5c0d-82f7-6eced020fb4f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198473}] :topic/math-academy-id 4139 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050879}] :topic/title "Differentiating Vector-Valued Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050289 :topic/id #uuid "3d4ec617-145f-5861-82b2-042ed58eeab2" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133830}] :topic/math-academy-id 2107 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853}] :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions"}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186713061 :question-generator/id #uuid "28ca4ebd-fbc8-5152-ae1d-f467460aa8e9" :question-generator/script "generators/13/137b050b987f2b8d8119d034f7466246c9a4e0c49d9a99ed4604daaf642944a9/generator.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134087 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f2ca5b8-5981-551e-84c2-42df6d6e6ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}1-{t}^{2}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257198} {:answer/id #uuid "553789d7-1249-5631-b43d-c24e6629b79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257510} {:answer/id #uuid "92587863-834e-5824-84e8-490bc42faa0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}-4{t}^{3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258073} {:answer/id #uuid "d7cc7618-4b33-520e-8e7b-a1238a933df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258674} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4592f9-7158-597f-b31d-611a63ed660a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "553789d7-1249-5631-b43d-c24e6629b79b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257510} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a824890-1de1-582c-a63f-8c8d8b7d97f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259188}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d06a9cd-a4fa-57a1-8abf-86189103f966" :question/math-academy-id "q-124638" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =({t}^{2})^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={t}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =({t}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & ={t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e8e2cc-41f8-50c1-a62f-8956a8ffa4b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256812} {:answer/id #uuid "3acca87f-62d5-5a56-9a81-2550fd4f1695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257301} {:answer/id #uuid "50b95edd-6999-5bc4-882b-895dba85827e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257471} {:answer/id #uuid "9910c73f-0dfb-563e-8d27-612cfca9fe3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258131} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f0d5a5-db21-59c8-919e-c65d835eac2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3acca87f-62d5-5a56-9a81-2550fd4f1695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257301} :answer-field/id #uuid "d82657a2-f036-50a5-95af-ce80d925cc34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259412}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2bc899a-43d8-534f-8ee7-8f110107ecc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124687" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c76c281-ee32-5011-a748-7ef89ba883b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256901} {:answer/id #uuid "0f2f834c-7360-5d7e-aa9f-f3b1d66dc200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256926} {:answer/id #uuid "8deb98b1-da50-5ad9-81ca-a8b82c4d3504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258032} {:answer/id #uuid "f026e5a0-af76-56a7-9bbf-b3adf93f8cf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258870} {:answer/id #uuid "f76be19f-342a-5624-9d5f-351998ae2615" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8deb98b1-da50-5ad9-81ca-a8b82c4d3504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258032} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1e2b23c-c022-536b-b1a7-a6999d60c1fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259371}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34976ea5-f60a-5f5a-a35d-aa51a7c27ec6" :question/math-academy-id "q-124679" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{sin}t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{sin}t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}t\\operatorname{sin}t+(\\operatorname{sin}t+1)\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7aa7a8e-9e40-5890-b907-1646c2bfa769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387556} {:answer/id #uuid "fb04b673-cfa2-5b12-b9a4-5988d0fccddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{2t}+1\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387557} {:answer/id #uuid "031aa6d1-de81-500a-bcbc-17b2b9631f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387558} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a0a90d-b0de-571b-a337-5b1debe5a8d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}+\\sqrt{t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387559} {:answer/id #uuid "95e15617-5463-564e-bb53-1c123c55a9b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{t}+2\\sqrt{t}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb04b673-cfa2-5b12-b9a4-5988d0fccddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{2t}+1\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387557} :answer-field/id #uuid "77226689-1668-509a-b6a9-0092f1ff6932" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387555}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dde4dac7-8853-5b97-ace9-e0b5cfb5ca89" :question/math-academy-id "q-124680" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)={e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}{(\\sqrt{t})}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(t{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}+2\\sqrt{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =t{e}^{2t}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t{e}^{2t}+1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b27d22ad-8705-5f6a-aff8-aa84df701016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387563} {:answer/id #uuid "617b9542-8b66-5bd1-849d-8670a94817ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387564} {:answer/id #uuid "7e9766f3-a9ca-5758-a584-fb9fe4c46f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387565} {:answer/id #uuid "c46e8e1a-fb23-5e40-9a39-b39f7180ab64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387566} {:answer/id #uuid "057e9216-5ff0-599a-887d-e7e4c1f3cf25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e9766f3-a9ca-5758-a584-fb9fe4c46f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387565} :answer-field/id #uuid "055cc4a0-b3d1-567f-9dfb-6c6033f4d5df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387562}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8250ae2f-e76d-56bd-9c2b-33447c40d270" :question/math-academy-id "q-124685" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the path $\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =(t\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot ((\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}t(\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t)+t\\operatorname{sin}t(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t+t{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\operatorname{cos}t) \\\\  & =t({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t) \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r})\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d74690-a384-593e-bbe9-04376d4a4e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}(- 6 t^{9} - 12 t^{7})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560135} {:answer/id #uuid "3639d3bd-81d9-59ee-80d7-aa90815fe75a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}(6 t^{9} + 12 t^{7})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560148} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb774a6-8143-5af6-85a1-87c3ef87a6c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}(- 3 t^{8} - 3 t^{4})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560156} {:answer/id #uuid "78b8be37-10a9-5547-b2d2-dfc48f0ba88a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}(- 6 t^{9})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560172} {:answer/id #uuid "c68080e7-9d27-5d7c-8a58-f7d3f4d56ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}(- 12 t^{7})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18d74690-a384-593e-bbe9-04376d4a4e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}(- 6 t^{9} - 12 t^{7})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560135} :answer-field/id #uuid "da95b72b-fda1-502c-abc3-44d5189f21b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560207}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07fde6e1-db88-5bf4-8158-d334fcb20915" :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 3 y^{2})\\,i+(- 3 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t^{4})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t^{2},\\,y=t^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- 3 y^{2})\\,i+(- 3 x^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 3 (t^{4})^{2})\\,i+(- 3 (t^{2})^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 3 t^{8})\\,i+(- 3 t^{4})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{4})j \\\\  & =(2 t)\\,i+(4 t^{3})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((- 3 t^{8})\\,i+(- 3 t^{4})\\,j)\\cdot ((2 t)\\,i+(4 t^{3})\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 3 t^{8})\\cdot (2 t)+(- 3 t^{4})\\cdot (4 t^{3}) \\\\  & =- 6 t^{9} - 12 t^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}(- 6 t^{9} - 12 t^{7})\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63dfcf58-d94d-58a9-bd78-835ccebe848c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}(- 2 t^{7})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560160} {:answer/id #uuid "83a28f66-266f-5100-9578-61fc83a68e76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}(- t^{6} + t^{4})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560175} {:answer/id #uuid "a672d544-45dc-5461-b619-de099625f753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}(3 t^{6})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560187} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ab7ec9-a6b7-561b-ab60-65feaf334e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}(- 2 t^{7} + 3 t^{6})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560199} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9575c6-9951-5e8d-9aee-31ad15591f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}(2 t^{7} - 3 t^{6})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3ab7ec9-a6b7-561b-ab60-65feaf334e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1}(- 2 t^{7} + 3 t^{6})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560199} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3e0851a-b569-52eb-98aa-bef0252a7519" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560205}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ccdc9c7-6ab9-5c96-8e88-01881ab4f8d8" :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(y^{2})\\,i+(- x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{3})\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t^{3},\\,y=t^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y^{2})\\,i+(- x^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =((t^{2})^{2})\\,i+(- (t^{3})^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(t^{4})\\,i+(- t^{6})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{3})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})j \\\\  & =(3 t^{2})\\,i+(2 t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((t^{4})\\,i+(- t^{6})\\,j)\\cdot ((3 t^{2})\\,i+(2 t)\\,j) \\\\  & =(t^{4})\\cdot (3 t^{2})+(- t^{6})\\cdot (2 t) \\\\  & =- 2 t^{7} + 3 t^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}(- 2 t^{7} + 3 t^{6})\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05566d8c-5ac6-549c-9f52-34432ca8bfda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}(- 12 t^{6})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560129} {:answer/id #uuid "2a3381c0-dd57-5ea5-80b0-588507cc251c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}(4 t^{7} + 12 t^{6})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560144} {:answer/id #uuid "5d072a4b-f302-5663-bfca-c9d9eaf34214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}(- 4 t^{7} - 12 t^{6})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560155} {:answer/id #uuid "a88e008e-4046-567e-aeeb-f0986bc7f33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}(- 2 t^{6} - 4 t^{4})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560188} {:answer/id #uuid "efc65d80-9483-54cf-aa11-17b24ec5d80c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}(- 4 t^{7})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d072a4b-f302-5663-bfca-c9d9eaf34214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{3}(- 4 t^{7} - 12 t^{6})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560155} :answer-field/id #uuid "3347fd80-340d-5376-971f-48237641ed1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560146}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "119c1b0e-aea6-5cde-bb9a-b98653f31898" :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r\n$$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 y^{2})\\,i+(- 4 x^{2})\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t^{2})\\,i+(t^{3})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,3]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t^{2},\\,y=t^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(- 2 y^{2})\\,i+(- 4 x^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 2 (t^{3})^{2})\\,i+(- 4 (t^{2})^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 2 t^{6})\\,i+(- 4 t^{4})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{3})j \\\\  & =(2 t)\\,i+(3 t^{2})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =((- 2 t^{6})\\,i+(- 4 t^{4})\\,j)\\cdot ((2 t)\\,i+(3 t^{2})\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 2 t^{6})\\cdot (2 t)+(- 4 t^{4})\\cdot (3 t^{2}) \\\\  & =- 4 t^{7} - 12 t^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F(r)\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_{0}^{3}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}(- 4 t^{7} - 12 t^{6})\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b37a832-4594-5202-b7fa-26249eaa2e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}(- t^{2} \\left(4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) - t \\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right))\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560136} {:answer/id #uuid "760b23ec-0be1-5eca-af76-0b9d0450d8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}(4 t^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - t \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560168} {:answer/id #uuid "7872e722-63ad-5d67-8768-6903fc04aea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}(- 7 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560170} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4788f9-df16-5eba-b506-df6695d62740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}(- 3 t^{2} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + t^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560173} {:answer/id #uuid "93c522b3-1274-5685-8439-2d136b4bd8af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}(t^{2} \\left(4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) + t \\left(- 3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right))\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93c522b3-1274-5685-8439-2d136b4bd8af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :a